meta {
  name: Store S3 credentials
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/schools/{{schoolId}}/profiles/s3
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "endpoint": "http://localhost:9000",
    "region": "us-east-1",
    "bucket": "emerge-dps-ngp",
    "access_key_id": "minioadmin",
    "secret_access_key": "minioadmin",
    "use_path_style": true
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.stored: eq true
}

docs {
  Encrypted the same way the postgres profile is: AES-256-GCM with the school id
  as additional authenticated data, plaintext never persisted and never returned.

  WITHOUT THIS REQUEST THE WHOLE ASSET LIFECYCLE IS UNREACHABLE. `POST
  /library/assets` answers 503 "this school has no object store configured" and
  writes no row, so `/assets/{id}/ready`, `/assets/{id}/link` and the asset
  delete have nothing to act on. That is why this sits in folder 02 rather than
  beside the asset requests in folder 10 - it is a precondition of that folder,
  not a member of it.

  The endpoint above is real on a stock checkout. `docker compose up -d
  object-store-bucket` starts MinIO on 9000 and creates the `emerge-dps-ngp`
  bucket before exiting; the body here is the compose defaults verbatim, so
  changing OBJECT_STORE_PORT or OBJECT_STORE_BUCKET in .env means changing this
  body to match. The full sequence has been run against it end to end: create
  asset, signed PUT, ready, download link, delete, and cmd/assetreap removing
  the object afterwards.

  The field is `use_path_style`, not `path_style`. The API refuses unknown
  fields outright - 422 "the body does not match the s3 credential shape" - so
  the near miss fails here rather than quietly defaulting to virtual-host
  addressing, which localhost cannot serve anyway.

  The presigned PUT that `POST /library/assets` returns binds three headers into
  the signature - x-amz-checksum-sha256, Content-Length and Content-Type - so a
  client that sends only the URL is refused. Against real S3 that refusal is
  SignatureDoesNotMatch; MinIO says the same thing differently, 400 AccessDenied
  "There were headers present in the request which were not signed". Either way
  the URL alone is not a credential for the upload. Bruno cannot perform that
  upload as part of this collection; the asset requests document the step rather
  than faking it.
}
